package Libraries.Game.Graphics.Trees

use Libraries.Compute.Matrix4
use Libraries.Compute.Math
use Libraries.Compute.Vector3
use Libraries.Containers.Number32BitArray
use Libraries.Game.BoundingBox

/*
CullingVolume3D is the region a camera or a light can see, as a set of planes
facing inward. A box is inside the volume if it is on the inner side of every
plane.

The planes are taken from a view-projection matrix, the matrix that turns a
world position into a position on the screen. A point is visible when, after
the matrix, its x and y are between -w and w and its z is no more than w.
Each of those five limits is one plane. Building the planes this way works
for any camera or light, left-handed or right-handed, perspective or
orthographic, because it uses only what the matrix does.

There is no near plane. OpenGL and Vulkan put it in different places (z of
-w or 0), and leaving it out means the volume does not need to know which
graphics system is running. For a camera, the four side planes already
reject everything behind it, so the only extra items are ones between the
camera and its near plane, a very small space. For a directional light's
shadows, the near plane must be left out anyway: items between the light and
its shadow area still cast shadows into it.

The library's Frustum class is not used. Its box test, BoundsInFrustum, is
commented out, and it builds its planes from corners that assume OpenGL's
depth range in a fixed order, which may no longer face inward in the
left-handed world.

Attribute: Example

use Libraries.Game.Graphics.Trees.CullingVolume3D
use Libraries.Compute.Matrix4
use Libraries.Game.BoundingBox
use Libraries.Compute.Vector3

// A new Matrix4 does nothing to a point, so this volume holds x and y
// from -1 to 1, and z up to 1.
Matrix4 matrix
CullingVolume3D volume
volume:Set(matrix)

Vector3 minimum
Vector3 maximum
minimum:Set(-0.5, -0.5, 0)
maximum:Set(0.5, 0.5, 0.5)
BoundingBox box
box:Set(minimum, maximum)
output volume:Test(box) = volume:INSIDE
*/
class CullingVolume3D
    public constant integer OUTSIDE = 0
    public constant integer INTERSECTING = 1
    public constant integer INSIDE = 2

    // Four numbers for each plane, a, b, c and d. A point (x, y, z) is on the
    // inner side when a * x + b * y + c * z + d >= 0. A Number32BitArray holds
    // plain numbers, where an Array<number> would wrap each one in an object.
    // 32 bits is the precision the graphics card works in anyway.
    Number32BitArray planes
    integer planeCount = 0

    // The same values as OUTSIDE, INTERSECTING and INSIDE, kept in fields.
    // Reading a constant is an action call, which Test would otherwise make
    // for every box it tests.
    integer outside = OUTSIDE
    integer intersecting = INTERSECTING
    integer inside = INSIDE
    Math math

    on create
        planes:SetSize(20)
    end

    /*
    This action sets the volume from a view-projection matrix, such as a
    camera's combined matrix or a light's shadow matrix.

    Attribute: Parameter matrix The view-projection matrix to read the planes
    from.
    */
    action Set(Matrix4 matrix)
        planeCount = 0

        // Left and right: -w <= x <= w.
        AddPlane(matrix:row3column0 + matrix:row0column0, matrix:row3column1 + matrix:row0column1,
            matrix:row3column2 + matrix:row0column2, matrix:row3column3 + matrix:row0column3)
        AddPlane(matrix:row3column0 - matrix:row0column0, matrix:row3column1 - matrix:row0column1,
            matrix:row3column2 - matrix:row0column2, matrix:row3column3 - matrix:row0column3)

        // Bottom and top: -w <= y <= w.
        AddPlane(matrix:row3column0 + matrix:row1column0, matrix:row3column1 + matrix:row1column1,
            matrix:row3column2 + matrix:row1column2, matrix:row3column3 + matrix:row1column3)
        AddPlane(matrix:row3column0 - matrix:row1column0, matrix:row3column1 - matrix:row1column1,
            matrix:row3column2 - matrix:row1column2, matrix:row3column3 - matrix:row1column3)

        // Far: z <= w.
        AddPlane(matrix:row3column0 - matrix:row2column0, matrix:row3column1 - matrix:row2column1,
            matrix:row3column2 - matrix:row2column2, matrix:row3column3 - matrix:row2column3)
    end

    /*
    This action sets the volume to the column of space that can cast a
    directional light's shadow within a distance of a point, such as the
    camera.

    The column is a square, twice the distance wide, centered on the point
    and running along the light's direction. Toward the light it has no
    end, so a tall object far toward the light is still inside it. Away
    from the light, it ends the distance past the point, since anything
    farther is behind whatever its shadow would fall on.

    Its five planes face inward, as those read from a matrix do: two at the
    distance on either side of the point, along a direction across the
    light; two the same, along the other direction across the light; and
    one the distance past the point, along the light.

    Attribute: Parameter direction The direction the light shines in. It
    does not need to be unit length.
    Attribute: Parameter x The x coordinate of the column's center.
    Attribute: Parameter y The y coordinate of the column's center.
    Attribute: Parameter z The z coordinate of the column's center.
    Attribute: Parameter distance How far the column reaches to each side,
    and past the center.
    Attribute: Returns False if the direction has no length. The volume is
    then left with no planes, and since a box is only outside when a plane
    rules it out, Test says every box is inside. Do not search with the
    volume when this returns false.
    */
    action SetShadowColumn(Vector3 direction, number x, number y, number z, number distance) returns boolean
        planeCount = 0

        number forwardX = direction:GetX()
        number forwardY = direction:GetY()
        number forwardZ = direction:GetZ()
        number length = math:SquareRoot(forwardX * forwardX + forwardY * forwardY + forwardZ * forwardZ)
        if length = 0
            return false
        end
        forwardX = forwardX / length
        forwardY = forwardY / length
        forwardZ = forwardZ / length

        // A direction across the light: up crossed with the light's
        // direction, using z as up if the light points nearly straight up
        // or down.
        number upX = 0
        number upY = 1
        number upZ = 0
        if math:AbsoluteValue(forwardY) > 0.99
            upY = 0
            upZ = 1
        end
        number rightX = upY * forwardZ - upZ * forwardY
        number rightY = upZ * forwardX - upX * forwardZ
        number rightZ = upX * forwardY - upY * forwardX
        number rightLength = math:SquareRoot(rightX * rightX + rightY * rightY + rightZ * rightZ)
        rightX = rightX / rightLength
        rightY = rightY / rightLength
        rightZ = rightZ / rightLength

        // The other direction across the light, at right angles to both.
        number sideX = forwardY * rightZ - forwardZ * rightY
        number sideY = forwardZ * rightX - forwardX * rightZ
        number sideZ = forwardX * rightY - forwardY * rightX

        // How far the center is along each direction.
        number rightAt = rightX * x + rightY * y + rightZ * z
        number sideAt = sideX * x + sideY * y + sideZ * z
        number forwardAt = forwardX * x + forwardY * y + forwardZ * z

        // A point is inside when each of these is 0 or more for it, as for
        // the planes read from a matrix. The first is the point's distance
        // along the right direction, minus the center's, plus the distance,
        // so it is 0 or more for points no more than the distance to the
        // left of the center. The others work the same way.
        AddPlane(rightX, rightY, rightZ, distance - rightAt)
        AddPlane(-rightX, -rightY, -rightZ, distance + rightAt)
        AddPlane(sideX, sideY, sideZ, distance - sideAt)
        AddPlane(-sideX, -sideY, -sideZ, distance + sideAt)
        AddPlane(-forwardX, -forwardY, -forwardZ, distance + forwardAt)
        return true
    end

    /*
    This action adds a plane, scaled so (a, b, c) has length 1. A plane with
    no direction, which a broken matrix can produce, is skipped. A point (x, y,
    z) is on the inner side of the plane when a * x + b * y + c * z + d is 0 or
    more.

    Attribute: Parameter a How much the plane depends on x.
    Attribute: Parameter b How much the plane depends on y.
    Attribute: Parameter c How much the plane depends on z.
    Attribute: Parameter d How far the plane is from the origin.
    */
    private action AddPlane(number a, number b, number c, number d)
        number length = math:SquareRoot(a * a + b * b + c * c)
        if length = 0
            return now
        end
        integer i = planeCount * 4
        planes:Set(i, a / length)
        planes:Set(i + 1, b / length)
        planes:Set(i + 2, c / length)
        planes:Set(i + 3, d / length)
        planeCount = planeCount + 1
    end

    /*
    This action tests a box against the volume and returns OUTSIDE,
    INTERSECTING or INSIDE.

    For each plane, the box's corner farthest along the plane's direction is
    tested first. If even that corner is on the outer side, the whole box is
    outside. Otherwise the opposite corner is tested. If it is on the outer
    side, the box crosses the plane. A box is inside only if it crosses no
    plane.

    Attribute: Parameter box The box to test, in world coordinates.
    Attribute: Returns OUTSIDE, INTERSECTING or INSIDE.
    */
    action Test(BoundingBox box) returns integer
        number minX = box:GetMinimum():GetX()
        number minY = box:GetMinimum():GetY()
        number minZ = box:GetMinimum():GetZ()
        number maxX = box:GetMaximum():GetX()
        number maxY = box:GetMaximum():GetY()
        number maxZ = box:GetMaximum():GetZ()

        boolean crosses = false
        integer p = 0
        repeat while p < planeCount
            integer i = p * 4
            number a = planes:Get(i)
            number b = planes:Get(i + 1)
            number c = planes:Get(i + 2)
            number d = planes:Get(i + 3)

            number farX = minX
            number nearX = maxX
            if a >= 0
                farX = maxX
                nearX = minX
            end
            number farY = minY
            number nearY = maxY
            if b >= 0
                farY = maxY
                nearY = minY
            end
            number farZ = minZ
            number nearZ = maxZ
            if c >= 0
                farZ = maxZ
                nearZ = minZ
            end

            if a * farX + b * farY + c * farZ + d < 0
                return outside
            end
            if a * nearX + b * nearY + c * nearZ + d < 0
                crosses = true
            end
            p = p + 1
        end

        if crosses
            return intersecting
        end
        return inside
    end

    /*
    This action returns how many planes the volume has: 5, or fewer if the
    matrix gave a plane with no direction.

    Attribute: Returns The number of planes.
    */
    action GetPlaneCount returns integer
        return planeCount
    end
end
